review_notes:
I reviewed all 75 lines: the definitions heavyPotential, heavyTrigger and heavyEdit, and the theorems heavyEdit_le, heavyFlag_at, heavy_stationary and heavy_mover. I also read HeavyProcess.run_stationary_payment and run_mover_payment in HeavyProcessEdits.lean.

The only candidate for repeated proof machinery is the pair heavy_stationary (lines 47–58) and heavy_mover (lines 60–72). Both proofs do the same four things:
- apply a HeavyProcess payment lemma to the same arguments (D.base, D.positive, a `show 2*Fintype.card X<Fintype.card (HeavySlot X)` side condition, D.heavyFlag, D.point, radiusStream and radiusStream_bounds);
- use `change` to restate it in Data vocabulary;
- rewrite with point_at/dist_comm;
- finish with `nlinarith only [h, D.heavyEdit_le n p ω]`.

The real mathematical content is already factored out. The stationary and mover payment bounds are separate lemmas in HeavyProcessEdits.lean, and the shared comparison between the edit indicator and editCharge is already the lemma heavyEdit_le, which both proofs reuse.

What remains repeated is argument plumbing: one cardinality side condition, the radius-stream bound argument, a `change` to unfold definitions and a one-line linear-arithmetic finish. The rubric excludes routine unfolding, short calculations and thin wrappers around existing API. A helper for the shared arguments would just be such a wrapper, and the two `change` targets differ because the source lemmas have different statements. So I am not reporting any finding.

Remaining uncertainty: I could not run Lean, so I did not test any alternative refactoring.
